{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Come, let us deal wisely with him.</b> הב\"ה has the numerical value 12 — נתחכמה לי\"ב, for the 12 tribes. And Rashi explained: he acted wisely against Israel's savior, etc. And they erred, for He swore only that He would not bring water upon the nations, but that the nations would not come in water — He did not swear. And the Sages said: three were in that counsel — Balaam, Job, and Jethro. Balaam, who counseled — was killed; Job, who was silent — was sentenced to suffering; Jethro, who fled — his descendants merited to sit in the Chamber of Hewn Stone. And Balaam, who was in the counsel of 'הבה נתחכמה,' is difficult against what we say in the chapter חלק: Bar thirty and three years was Balaam a laden ass when Phineas killed him the brigand, because it is written: ״אנשי דמים ומרמה לא יחצו ימיהם.״ And if he was in this counsel, he would have been one hundred and forty years old when he fell in the war against Midian — for at the very least he was twenty when he counseled, and reckon Moses' eighty years and the forty years they travelled in the desert — the war against Midian was then, as it is written ״אחר תאסף,״ behold he was 140 years old. Therefore R. Menachem of Dampierre explained: three were in that counsel of ״לכה איעצך,״ as we say: he said to Balak, their El is a hater of promiscuity, as is stated in חלק. And the Rav Moses raised a difficulty, for in Divrei HaYamim of Moses you find written the explanation of the counsel here: ״ויען בלעם הקוסם.״ And therefore R. Menachem raised a difficulty from what we say in זבחים, chapter פרת חטאת: at the time the Holy One, blessed be He, gave the Torah to Israel, all the nations gathered around Balaam and said: what is this sound of the multitude that we have heard — perhaps the Holy One, blessed be He, wants to bring a flood, as is stated there. And even if you should say he was only twenty years old when they ask him for counsel, and at the end of forty [years] he was killed — then he was sixty when he died. And there are manuscripts in which is written: ״ונקבצו כלן אצל קמואל,״ and these words are not clear to me, for there it implies that Kemuel father of Aram — that is Balaam; if so, he would be deficient [in years] at his death. And also it is found in Bereshit Rabbah: Kemuel father of Aram — that is Laban the Aramean; that is Cushan-Rish'ataim; that is Balaam. And why was he called Kemuel? Because he rose up (קם) against the nation (אומה) of El. And so too we say in חלק: he is Balaam, he is Be'or. And why was he called Be'or? Because he had relations with his beast (בעירו). He is Laban; he is Cushan-Rish'ataim. And why was his name called Cushan-Rish'ataim? Because he did two wickednesses (רשעיות) — one in the days of Jacob and one in the days of Judges. From all these it follows that he was deficient [in years]. And that which is written in Job ״כי יתרי פתח ויענני״ — and in the Masoretic text it is written יתרו, and they explain: this is what it says — Jethro too was in that counsel, and it was he who opened the matter: ״ואותי תענה.״ And it is difficult, for we say that Jethro fled, whereas Job did not flee but was silent. And one may answer that Jethro was the great man of his generation at that time, and it was in his power to protest, and he did not — but fled; therefore they hold him accountable, whereas Job [was not held accountable]."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The text is densely elliptical throughout, with multiple nested citations and unresolved abbreviations. 'פלגינן' or similar for 'deficient in years' is uncertain; read as תפל (deficient). The final comparison between Jethro and Job's culpability turns on whether silence vs. flight vs. failure to protest carries different moral weight; rendered hyper-literally to preserve the compilers' compressed logic."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:02:38.843092375Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}